\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Unitary gauge for one sector labeling}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Fix a \vocabk{sector labeling}{fdip-0121} #{\lambda}. For each
label #{i} choose a section #{\orbitlift_i:\mathcal O_i\to G} with
#{\orbitlift_i(o)\phi^\circ_i=o} and #{\orbitlift_i(\phi^\circ_i)=1}.
The frame at #{x} is #{g_x=\orbitlift_{\lambda(x)}(\phi_x)}, and the
reduced link is #{\check U_e=g_{h(e)}^{-1}U_e g_{s(e)}}. Define
##{\sigma_i(a,o)=\orbitlift_i(ao)^{-1}a\,\orbitlift_i(o).}
Then #{\sigma_i(a,o)\in G^\circ_i}. Retaining the scalar and
replacing every link by its reduced link is a bijection from the
joint configurations of #{\lambda} to
#{\prod_x\mathcal O_{\lambda(x)}\times G^E}. Under a vertex gauge
#{a\in G^X} the reduced links transform by
##{\check U_e\longmapsto
 \sigma_{\lambda(h(e))}(a_{h(e)},\phi_{h(e)})\,
 \check U_e\,
 \sigma_{\lambda(s(e))}(a_{s(e)},\phi_{s(e)})^{-1}.}
Joint orbits of #{G^X} are in bijection with orbits of
#{\prod_x G^\circ_{\lambda(x)}} acting on #{G^E}. The #{k}-algebra
of invariant functions of a joint configuration is isomorphic to
##{k^{\,G^E/\prod_x G^\circ_{\lambda(x)}}.}
The one-orbit case is \ref{fdip-011E}. On a fixed-point orbit the
section value is #{1} and the residual factor at that vertex is
#{G}.}

\proof{
\p{The inverse bijection recovers the frames from the retained
scalar and sets #{U_e=g_{h(e)}V_e g_{s(e)}^{-1}}. Applying
#{\sigma_i(a,o)} to #{\phi^\circ_i} returns #{\phi^\circ_i}, so the
cocycle value lies in the stabilizer. The gauged frame at #{x} is
#{a_x g_x\sigma_{\lambda(x)}(a_x,\phi_x)^{-1}}, and substitution
gives the displayed endpoint action. Any other normalized section
of orbit #{i} has the form #{\orbitlift_i(o)a^\circ(o)} with
#{a^\circ(o)\in G^\circ_i} and #{a^\circ(\phi^\circ_i)=1}. It
changes a reduced link only by the endpoint action of those
stabilizer values. Thus the residual orbit does not depend on the
choice of sections.}

\p{The vertex gauge with value #{g_x^{-1}} at each site carries
#{(U,\phi)} to the reduced connection and the reference scalar of
#{\lambda}. Two such reference configurations lie in one joint
orbit exactly when a vertex gauge fixes every reference scalar and
carries one link tuple to the other, which means the gauge lies in
#{\prod_x G^\circ_{\lambda(x)}}. This is the orbit bijection. An
invariant function is constant on joint orbits, so restriction to
the reference scalar assigns one value to each residual orbit, and
every such assignment extends uniquely by constancy on the joint
orbit. Restriction preserves pointwise sums and products. If
#{G^\circ_i=G}, then #{\mathcal O_i} has one point, the normalized
section sends it to #{1}, and the residual factor at each vertex
labeled #{i} is #{G}.}
}
